      "kind": "item",
      "label": "(coherence) Where does a folder's list of people live?",
      "excerpt": "(coherence) Where does a folder's list of people live?",
      "text": "what is \"per folder\" here?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-04T13:05:55.910Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-04T13:08:14.953Z",
          "html": "<epic-reply from=\"Claude\" at=\"2026-11-04 08:15\" re=\"C1\">\n  <p>You asked:  <q>what is \"per folder\" here?</q></p>\n  <p>A <b>data folder</b> is epic <code>data</code>'s word for one program's saved rows, kept together under one address.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The family cookbook's recipes are one data folder, at <code>ana@spell/family-cookbook</code>.</li>\n    <li>Ana's own recipe box, which nobody else sees, is another, at <code>ana@spell/recipes</code>.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>\"One list of people per folder\" means each of those carries its own list of who may read and write it.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The cookbook's list holds Ana, Bo and Grandma;  the recipe box's holds only Ana.</li>\n    <li>Nothing finer than that:  the list doesn't say \"Bo may edit only his own recipes\".</li>\n    <li>It matches a Space one to one:  one data folder becomes one Space, and its list becomes the Space's member list.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>Q4 only asks where that list is kept:  in the folder's own file (A), or as membership records in Spell's people library (B).</p>\n  <epic-net-effect>\n    <p>\"Per folder\" means per program's data, such as the cookbook, not per folder on disk.</p>\n  </epic-net-effect>\n</epic-reply>"
        },
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "at": "2026-11-04T13:15:37.074Z",
          "text": "Let's say \"project data folder\"",
          "edited": "2026-11-04T13:15:39.933Z"
        }
      ],
